# A hand-written sentiment set, matched pair by pair against the synthetic one.
#
# Every subject appears under both labels, so a probe that separates these has
# to be reading the sentiment rather than the topic. Read the left edge of the
# file to check that: the sigils should alternate, not clump.

name: sentiment-handwritten
labels: negative, positive
notes: balanced, and no subject appears under only one label
source: written by hand; 24 examples is too few to train a good probe, so read it rather than score it

+ I loved every minute of the film
- I hated every minute of the film

+ The concert was worth the drive and then some
- The concert was not worth the drive, or the ticket

+ We left the restaurant already planning to come back
- We left the restaurant early and paid for the privilege

+ The museum is small, and every room in it earns its place
- The museum is small, and somehow still had nothing in it

+ A play I would happily sit through twice
- A play I could barely sit through once

+ The hotel staff remembered our names by the second morning
- The hotel staff never quite managed to find our booking

+ The exhibition changed how I look at the subject
- The exhibition told me nothing I did not already know

+ Her lecture was the only one I took notes in
- Her lecture was the only one I fell asleep in

+ The festival ran late and nobody minded
- The festival ran late and everybody minded

+ Our guide on the tour clearly loved the place
- Our guide on the tour clearly wanted to be elsewhere

+ The recital ended and the room stayed quiet for a moment
- The recital ended and the room emptied immediately

+ The workshop was three hours that felt like one
- The workshop was one hour that felt like three
